{{Quote Box|style=float:none; max-width:none|''There is nothing else but philosophy to a dovah. It is no accident that we do battle with our Thu'um, our Voices. There is no distinction between debate and combat to a dragon. Tinvaak los grah. For us it is one and the same.''|[[Lore:Paarthurnax|Paarthurnax]]{{ref|name=Paarthurnax}}}}</noinclude>
[[Lore:Dragon|Dragons]] (also known as '''drakes''',{{ref|name=OATD}} '''wyrms''' or '''worms''',{{ref|{{Cite Book|The Story of Lyrisius}}}} '''dov''' or '''dovah''' in [[Lore: Dragon Language|their native language]], '''drah-gkon''' or '''dov-rha''' to the ancient [[Lore:Nord|Nord]]s, or '''Aka''' in [[Lore:Ehlnofex|Ehlnofex]]){{ref|name=MA}}{{ref|name=APAYQ|{{Cite Book|Artorius Ponticus Answers Your Questions}}}} are a rarely-seen [[Lore:Race|race]] of large [[Lore:Reptile|reptilian]] beasts of [[Lore:Aedra|Aedric]] nature.{{ref|name=TDW|{{Cite Book|The Dragon War}}}}{{ref|name=TBD|{{Cite Book|There Be Dragons}}}} They are rumored to be from [[Lore:Akavir|Akavir]] (which literally means "Dragon Land"),{{ref|{{Cite Book|PGE|3|Other Lands}}}} though there are ancient tales of Dragons also coming from [[Lore:Atmora|Atmora]].{{ref|name=TDW}}{{ref|name=TBD}}{{ref|name=MA|{{Cite Book|Mysterious Akavir}}}} They are large, scaled creatures, easily several times larger than a human or elf. They have long, slender extremities, with thin bat-like wings and ridges of spikes along their back. Dragons have three sharp talons and one vestigial digit known as a dewclaw on each of their feet, as evidenced by the way their [[Lore:Dragon Alphabet|alphabet]] is written.{{ref|name=WW|[[SR:Word Wall|Word Walls]] in ''[[SR:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}{{ref|name=SDS|group=UOL|[http://www.gameinformer.com/b/features/archive/2011/01/20/skyrim-s-dragon-shouts.aspx?PostPageIndex&#61;3 Skyrim's Dragon Shouts], GameInformer, January 20, 2011}} There are several varieties of Dragon that come in a range of colors.{{ref|name=MA}}

They are regularly said to breathe fire, but more accurately, they speak it. They speak words in their language a certain way, known as a [[Lore:Thu'um|thu'um]] or "Shout", to cause many effects, including elemental breaths such as fire or frost.{{ref|name=Arngeir|[[Skyrim:Arngeir|Arngeir]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}

Dragons are well-known for their affinity for [[Lore:Magic|magic]].{{ref|name=TBD}} It is unknown how they possess the ability to speak and fly despite their lack of lips or the fact that their wings are not naturally strong enough to support such heavy torsos.{{ref|name=DLMNM|{{Cite Book|Dragon Language: Myth no More}}}}{{ref|name=KE12|{{Cite Book|King Edward, Part XII}}}} As the immortal children of [[Lore:Akatosh|Akatosh]], they are specially attuned to the flow of time, and they feel an innate urge to dominate others that is near-impossible to overcome.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|[[Skyrim:Paarthurnax (dragon)|Paarthurnax]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Some sources suggest both Dragons and [[Lore:Dragonborn|Dragonborn]] are fragments of Akatosh's [[Lore:Souls|soul]].{{ref|name=VonosJournal|{{Cite Book|Vonos' Journal}}}}{{FMI}}<noinclude>

Although Dragons are immortal,{{ref|name=TBD}} their physical form can be destroyed. Although they may appear to have been killed, Dragons defeated in such a manner are not truly dead and can rise again.{{ref|name=Blade|[[SR:A Blade in the Dark|A Blade in the Dark]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} In the [[Lore:Second Era|Second Era]], scholars still intensely debated the qualities of [[Lore:Dragon Souls|Dragon souls]], with some believing that their tethers to bodily remains were stronger than that of mortals, which raised the ominous possibility that they might be resurrected.{{ref|name=LADitSE|{{Cite Book|Loremaster's Archive - Dragons in the Second Era}}}} This concern was ultimately validated in {{Year|4E 201}}, when [[Lore:Alduin|Alduin]] reappeared in [[Lore:Skyrim|Skyrim]] and began resurrecting his kin one by one.{{ref|name=Blade}}

However, a Dragon may not be resurrected if their soul is consumed by a fellow Dragon.{{ref|name=Arngeir}} This grants the recipient a portion of the knowledge and life essence of their fallen opponent, but it also destroys the Dragon permanently, rendering it beyond the ability of any ritual to resurrect.{{ref|[[SR:Balgruuf the Greater|Balgruuf the Greater]]'s dialogue}}{{ref|name=Paarthurnax}} This ability also extends to the Dragonborn, mortals born with the soul of a Dragon who can become the greatest of Dragonslayers by absorbing the souls of their quarry and thereby steal their power.{{ref|name=Esbern|[[SR:Esbern|Esbern]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}{{ref|name=TBOTD|{{Cite Book|The Book of the Dragonborn}}}} It is also possible to use powerful [[Lore:Soul Magic|soul magic]] to sever the connection between a Dragon's soul and its physical remains, although the effects of this is the subject of fierce scholarly debate, with some speculating that a Dragon soul once severed may simply dissolve over time or return to join Father Akatosh.{{ref|name=LADitSE}}

==History==
===Dawn Era===
====Mythic Origins====
[[File:LG-cardart-Alduin 02.jpg|thumb|left|Alduin, first born of Akatosh]]{{Quote Box|style=float:none; max-width:none|''There is no credible story of how dragons came to be. According to dremora that the College of Whispers have "questioned," they just were, and are. Eternal, immortal, unchanging, and unyielding. They are not born or hatched. They do not mate or breed.''|''[[Lore:There Be Dragons|There Be Dragons]]''}}
Akatosh is said to be the father of Dragons, whom he made in his image.{{ref|name=TDW}}{{ref|name=VOF|{{Cite Book|Varieties of Faith...}}}} His avatar often takes the form of a golden or flaming Dragon.{{ref|[[Oblivion:Light the Dragonfires|Light the Dragonfires]] quest in ''[[OB:Oblivion|Oblivion]]''}}{{ref|[[Castles:Avatar of Akatosh|Avatar of Akatosh]] in ''[[Castles:Castles|Castles]]''}} According to Archmage [[Lore:Shalidor|Shalidor]], Dragons are in some manner kindred of the Time God, either his children, or lesser relations, or part of him that split off when time began.{{ref|name=SI|{{Cite Book|Shalidor's Insights}}}}

Dragons are said to be eternal, immortal, unchanging, and unyielding.{{ref|name=TBD}} According to the writings of Archmage Shalidor, Dragons had existed since before the advent of sequential time in the [[Lore:Dawn Era|Dawn Era]],{{Ref|name=DTRaP|{{Cite Book|Dread Their Return and Prepare}}}}{{Ref|name=AMOVAB|{{Cite Book|A Matter of Voice and Brass: Dragon Bones DLC Interview|ns_base=GEN}}}} but were initially as wild as anything else. It was [[Lore:Alduin|Alduin]], Firstborn of Akatosh, who created their civilization.{{ref|name=SI|{{Cite Book|Shalidor's Insights}}}} It is said the Dragons ruled over all of [[Lore:Mundus|Mundus]] before the birth of men.{{ref|name=Hrothgar|[[Skyrim:High Hrothgar#Etched Tablets|Etched Tablets]] of High Hrothgar in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Nordic legend says that, in these early years, the Dragons were the only creatures who could harness the power of their voice for great feats of magic, though they only did so in times of "True Need".{{ref|name=Hrothgar}} Despite Dragons supposedly existing before time was a concept, some Dragons are described as being more ancient than others, with Alduin and [[Lore:Paarthurnax|Paarthurnax]] considered the oldest by other Dragons,{{Ref|name=Odahviing|[[SR:Odahviing|Odahviing]]'s dialogue in ''[[SR:Dragonslayer#The Obituary|Skyrim]]''}} hence why not all of them are considered equal in power.{{ref|name=Loading}}{{Ref|name=ESONahviintaas|[[ON:Nahviintaas|Nahviintaas]]' dialogue in [[ON:The Return of Alkosh|ESO]]}}

Some cultures are known to have their own myths concerning the origin of Dragons, though it ultimately remains a matter shrouded in ambiguity. 

According to a Khajiiti myth, [[Lore:Akha|Akha]] embarked on a journey across the world, following his father [[Lore:Ahnurr|Ahnurr]]'s advice to seek love. He mated with beings from distant lands, including the [[Lore:Winged Serpent of the East|Winged Serpent of the East]], the [[Lore:Dune Queen of the West|Dune Queen of the West]], and the [[Lore:Mother Mammoth of the North|Mother Mammoth of the North]], resulting in numerous draconic offspring. After Akha disappeared in the South, [[Lore:Alkosh|Alkosh]] emerged in his place and took over the role and crown of the First Cat, assuming rule over Akha's children and the myriad kingdoms of Akha along the [[Lore:Many Paths|Many Paths]]. However, he was eventually [[Lore:Dragon Break|overthrown]], and his body was scattered on the West Wind. [[Lore:Khenarthi|Khenarthi]] intervened to restore Alkosh, flying across the Many Paths and putting him back together, and the two now watch over the Many Paths and safeguard them from the wayward children of Akha.{{Ref|name=TWS|{{Cite Book|The Wandering Spirits}}}}{{intnote|nb1|[nb 1]}}

According to the transcribed creation myth of the [[Lore:Adzi-Kostleel|Adzi-Kostleel]] tribe of [[Lore:Argonian|Argonians]], [[Lore:Anu|Atak]] the Great Root and [[Lore:Padomay|Kota]] the Serpent fought until they merged into Atakota. It shed its roots and skin, spoke "[[Lore:Aurbis|Maybe]]", and produced a hungry Shadow from the discarded skin. Atakota devoured worlds while the absence of conflict allowed things to begin and end. The Shadow eventually slept, while spirits grew until their worlds became overcrowded. They attacked Atakota and drank its blood, separating Atak and Kota and renewing their conflict. Amid this chaos, some spirits drank deeply of Atakota's blood and sap, and they grew scales, fangs, and wings, and forgot purposes beyond devouring. Kota's blood formed oceans and Atak's sap formed stones. The roots eventually appealed to the Shadow, whose intervention ended the chaos.{{Ref|name=COTR|{{Cite Book|Children of the Root}}}}

Whatever the case, Dragons have been known to refer to the Time God as their "father", and have used both the name "Akatosh" and the name "Alkosh" to name their progenitor.{{Ref|name=Nahfahlaar|[[ON:Nahfahlaar|Nahfahlaar]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Dragonhold|Dragonhold]]''}}{{ref|name=Paarthurnax}}{{Ref|name=Alduin|[[Skyrim:Alduin|Alduin]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}{{Ref|name=Kaalgrontiid|[[ON:Kaalgrontiid|Kaalgrontiid]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}}

===Merethic Era===
====Alduin's Reign====
[[File:SR-quest-Alduin's Bane 13.jpg|thumb|left|Alduin Cast into Time]]
Alduin's leadership over the other Dragons would eventually extend to mankind in the [[Lore:Merethic Era|Merethic Era]]. This [[Lore:Dragon Cult|Dragon Cult]] originated in [[Lore:Atmora|Atmora]] but would eventually come south to [[Lore:Tamriel|Tamriel]] where the humans were lorded over with a much crueler hand.{{ref|name=TDW|{{Cite Book|The Dragon War}}}} Beyond the more understood-to-history rule over Tamriel and Atmora, the Dragons at the height of their power were said to rule the whole [[Lore:Nirn|world]].{{ref|name=TGATT|{{Cite Book|The Guardian and the Traitor}}}} Though all Dragons swore fealty to Alduin,{{ref|name=Arngeir}} his younger brother Paarthurnax served as his chief lieutenant and right hand.{{Ref|name=Esbern}}  Others, like [[Lore:Kaalgrontiid|Kaalgrontiid]] traveled further south, seeking to establish their own kingdoms outside of Alduin's direct supervision.{{Ref|name=LADitSE}} Under the Dragons were certain humans who held a special connection with the Dragons known as the [[Lore:Dragon Priest|Dragon Priests]].{{ref|name=TDW|}} Mortal servants occupied the lowest rank under these cruel masters.{{ref|name=TDW|}} This Dragon empire's center of power in Skyrim is thought to have been [[Lore:Bromjunaar|Bromjunaar]], where Dragon priests decided matters of their law.{{ref|name=AMM|{{Cite Book|A Minor Maze}}}}

Nordic legend holds that Alduin led his brethren and their once-benevolent minions to terrorize and subjugate the mortal races, triggering a rebellion.{{ref|name=TDW}}{{ref|name=DLMNM}} The Dragons began indiscriminately slaughtering them under the orders of [[Lore:Alduin|Alduin]], who had chosen to forsake his proper duties and conquer the land.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax}}{{ref|name=TAAD|{{Cite Book|The Alduin/Akatosh Dichotomy}}}} On [[Lore:Solstheim|Solstheim]], the Dragon Priest [[Lore:Miraak|Miraak]] rebelled against his Draconic masters and displayed their remains as trophies. The Dragons retaliated by razing his temple.{{Ref|name=DBFrea|[[Skyrim:Frea|Frea]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Dragonborn|Dragonborn]]''}}

During this [[Lore:Dragon War|Dragon War]], mortals found ways to kill the Dragons, who were previously thought undefeatable.{{ref|name=TDW}}{{ref|name=TBD}} Some people, notably priests of the [[Lore:Nine Divines|Nine Divines]], claim that Akatosh intervened against the Dragons to help end the war, and that some Dragons fought against their own kind.{{ref|name=TDW}}{{ref|name=AOD}} Paarthurnax took pity on the Nords (purportedly at the request of [[Lore:Kyne|Kyne]]), and instructed several in the ways of the thu'um so that they could channel the power of their own voices using the [[Lore:Dragon Language|tongue of the Dragons]].{{ref|name=Paarthurnax}} These first Tongues (masters of the Voice) used their new power to turn the tide of the war, and they eventually "shout[ed] Alduin out of the world".{{ref|name=TDW}} The ancient Tongues created "Dragonrend", a Shout that forced Dragons to experience mortality. They used it to incapacitate Alduin briefly, but ultimately had to cast him into time with an [[Lore:Elder Scrolls|Elder Scroll]].{{Ref|name=Paarthurnax}}{{Ref|name=Skyrim1}} Many of the remaining Dragons who did not show fealty to mortals were slaughtered, clearing the way for the many empires which have risen and fallen since.{{ref|name=TDW}}{{ref|name=AOD}} Surviving followers of the Dragon Cult built burial mounds for Dragons slain during the Dragon War. They believed the Dragons would rise again and reward the faithful.{{Ref|name=TDW}} 
{{NewLeft}}

====Khunzar-ri and the Demons====
[[File:ON-statue-Halls of Colossus Bas Reliefs.png|thumb|A series of [[Lore:Statues|bas reliefs]] depicting Khunzar-ri's battle with Dragons, and their imprisonment in the Halls of Colossus]]
In the Merethic Era,{{ref|name=ESOAbnurTharn|[[Online:Abnur Tharn|Abnur Tharn]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]}} when [[Lore:Elsweyr|Elsweyr]] was composed of sixteen kingdoms, Kaalgrontiid and his rage of Dragons came to dominate the land, seeking to consume the [[Lore:Moons|Moons]]' lunar power. To stop them, the Khajiit hero [[Lore:Khunzar-ri|Khunzar-ri]] recruited four companions, [[Lore:Nurarion the Perfect|Nurarion the Perfect]], [[Lore:Flinthild Demon-Hunter|Flinthild Demon-Hunter]], [[Lore:Anequina Sharp-Tongue|Anequina Sharp-Tongue]], and [[Lore:Cadwell|Sir Cadwell]].{{Ref|name=KatD|{{Cite Book|Khunzar-ri and the Demons}}}} When fighting them proved to be futile, Cadwell suggested that it may be best to convince the Dragons that they were willing to aid them in their plans. In preparation, Anequina [[Lore:Shadow Dance|attuned]] to [[Lore:Shadow Dance Temple|Shadow Dance Temple]], making the moons align to form an eclipse, allowing the [[Lore:Moon Gate of Anequina|Moon Gate of Anequina]] to open a portal the [[Lore:Plane of Jode|Plane of Jode]], where the object the Dragons desired, [[Lore:Jode's Core|Jode's Core]], rested. The Dragons' ambitions would be their undoing; when the Dragons accessed the Plane of [[Lore:Jode|Jode]], Khunzar-ri convinced them to not simply absorb the power of the core like they originally intended, but instead store their energy within it in the guise that they would combine the lunar power with their own.{{ref|name=ESOCadwell}}{{ref|name=ESOKhamira}} The gamble worked, and the Dragons were weakened enough for them to retreat back into the wilderness.{{Ref|name=KatD}} To finish the job, Khunzar-ri once again convinced the Dragons to play a part of his scheme. He managed to lure them into the [[Lore:Halls of Colossus|Halls of Colossus]], where he managed to seal them away. In the aftermath, Khunzar-ri wrote on the legendary [[Lore:Wrathstone|Wrathstone]] in an ancient Ta'agra dialect, writings detailing the cartography of the location of the Demon Weapon. The Wrathstone itself is however much older than the Ta'agra writings.{{ref|name=ESOTharayya|[[Online:Tharayya|Tharayya]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Wrathstone|Wrathstone]]''}} The Dragons would be obscured as "demons" in legends, and become the fabled "Demon Weapon". Their power would be coveted by many people.{{Ref|name=KatD}}

====Akaviri Dragon Wars====
The [[Lore:Tsaesci (race)|Tsaesci]] of Akavir were known to hunt Dragons throughout their history, fighting in Dragon Wars in the times before the First Era.{{Ref|name=AFDOE}}{{Ref|name=Dragonfire Wolf}} At some point long before the Second Era, they sought to "eat" all of the Dragons. The Black Dragons managed to escape them into [[Lore:Ka Po' Tun (place)|Po Tun]] but they successfully enslaved the Red Dragons, and went on to use them as war mounts.{{ref|name=MA}}{{ref|name=PGE1Cyro}}{{Ref|name=Kiseravi|[[ON:Kiseravi|Kiseravi]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]&#58; ''[[Online:Elsweyr (DLC)|Elsweyr]]''}} However, after a war with the [[Lore:Ka Po' Tun (race)|tiger people]], the Dragons of Akavir were almost entirely wiped out.{{ref|name=MA}} Many of the surviving Dragons fled to Tamriel, which led to the theories that all of Tamriel's Dragons had originally come from Akavir.{{ref|name=RiseAndFall}}

At some point after this, the tiger people tried to become the Dragons. It is rumored that [[Lore:Tosh Raka|Tosh Raka]], their leader, was the first to succeed. He is said to now be the largest Dragon in the world.{{ref|name=MA}}

===First Era===
====Declining Influence====
[[File:CT-decoration-Dragon Painting (Cropped).png|right|thumb|First Era painting of a maiden with a Red Dragon]]
{{Quote Box|style=float:none; max-width:none; margin-left:0|''Oh, this was thousands of years ago, before the Akaviri crusaders cleansed Skyrim of dragons. Skyrim's mountains were still infested with dragons. One of the early Nord Kings caught a dragon and kept it in Dragonsreach.''|[[Lore:Esbern|Esbern]]{{ref|name=Esbern}}}} 
After the Dragon Cult's decline, surviving Dragons withdrew to remote places.{{ref|name=TDW}} They are thought to have favored solitary mountain lairs, sheltered from thieves and aggressors.{{ref|name=OATD|{{Cite Book|Olaf and the Dragon}}}}{{ref|name=Esbern}} Some Dragons even took up roost in occupied caverns, often kicking out the current inhabitants in the process.{{Ref|name=ESOYisareh|Dialogue with [[ON:Yisareh|Yisareh]] in [[Online:Fang Lair|Fang Lair]]}} According to some claims, Dragons were unable to settle in [[Lore:Morrowind|Morrowind]] due to being preyed upon by [[Lore:Skylamp|skylamps]],{{Ref|name=PoM|{{Cite Book|People of Morrowind|ns_base=GEN}}}}{{ref|name=GN|group=UOL}} or overwhelmed by [[Lore:Cliff Racer|cliff racers]].{{ref|name=GN|group=UOL|[[General: Gary Noonan's Posts|Gary Noonan's Posts]]}} 

In contrast, the fictionalized account of [[Lore:Edward|King Edward]] describes Dragons of the early First Era as openly living in and around isolated mountain villages. The villages were purportedly situated in areas that allowed flight free of obstructions as well as flats for raising cattle.{{ref|name=KE|{{Cite Book|King Edward}}}} A painting of a maiden with a tame red Dragon was known to have been created during the 16th century of the First Era.{{ref|name=DrPaint|[[Castles:Dragon Painting|Dragon Painting]] description in ''[[Castles:Castles|Castles]]''}} 

Some sources allege that a few Dragons may have sworn fealty to Nordic kings in the First Era. [[Lore:Dov-rha Axethanes|Dov-rha Axethanes]] wore armor gifted by Dragons in a time when Dragons and mortals took to the skies as bonded battle-mates.{{ref|name=Dov-rha Sabatons}}
One obscure account mentions several Dragons being involved in the [[Lore:All Flags Navy|All Flags Navy]]'s invasion of [[Lore:Thras|Thras]].{{ref|group=UOL|{{Cite Book|Nemon's Memorystone|ns_base=GEN}}}}

====Hunters from the East====
In the late First Era, the Tsaesci continued their crusade against the Dragons in Tamriel, following those that fled there.{{ref|name=RiseAndFall|{{Cite Book|The Rise and Fall of the Blades}}}} After their invasion of Tamriel and surrendering to [[Lore:Reman|Reman]] around {{Year|1E 2703}}, the [[Lore:Dragonguard|Akaviri Dragonguard]] was formed, dedicated to hunting Dragons.{{ref|name=TBOTD}} Additionally, Akaviri Dragon motifs and the practice of creating armor from Dragonscale would become common in Imperial culture. Their war mounts, the Red Dragons, would also come to [[Lore:Imperial Dragon|symbolize the Empire itself]].{{ref|name=PGE1Cyro}} Reman eventually stopped making sport of their kind, though certain hunters many years after his time sought out Dragons to slay them, such as the group that found [[Lore:Thurvokun|Thurvokun]].{{Ref|name=ESOUlfnor|[[ON:Ulfnor|Ulfnor]]'s dialogue in [[ESO:Online|ESO]]}}

====The Demon from the East====
[[File:ON-tapestry-The Dragonguard.jpg|right|thumb|Ja'darri and the Dragonguard confronting the Black Beast]]
Sometime in the First Era after the formation of the Dragonguard, the Dragon threat re-emerged in Elsweyr. The hero [[Lore:Ja'darri|Ja'darri]] formed an alliance with Reman's Dragonguard and the red Dragon [[Lore:Nahfahlaar|Nahfahlaar]]. She was tasked by Alkosh to use the [[Lore:Mask of Alkosh|Mask of Alkosh]] to extinguish a creature that threatened to unravel all of time: [[Lore:Laatvulon|Laatvulon]], a Black Dragon known as the Demon from the East.{{ref|name=ESOJD|[[ON:Ja'darri|Ja'darri]]'s dialogue during [[ON:The Dragonguard|The Dragonguard]] in [[ON:Dragonhold|ESO]]}} Ja'darri petitioned Nahfahlaar to imbue the Mask of Alkosh with his power to help defeat his ancient foe Laatvulon.{{ref|name=Nahfahlaar}} In an act he would later regret, he refused, leaving the Dragonguard to fight Laatvulon with limited power.{{ref|name=Nahfahlaar}} They would ultimately succeed in imprisoning Laatvulon but it cost Ja'darri her life.{{ref|name=ESONuziwa|[[Online:Moon-Priest Nuziwa|Moon-Priest Nuziwa]]'s dialogue in [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Dragonhold|Dragonhold]]''}}{{ref|name=Nahfahlaar}} Upon death, she was said to join Alkosh in a [[Lore:Spilled Sand|realm]] beyond even the [[Lore:Sands Behind the Stars|Sands Behind the Stars]].

Ja'darri's tomb has ancient tablets telling of four [[Lore:Khajiit#Khajiiti_Pantheon|Khajiiti spirits]] stepping in to aid Elsweyr. It claims that [[Lore:Azurah|Azurah]] saw the coming of Laatvulon, and responded by opening the Crimson Gate so that her children could make their stand. It additionally mentions Alkosh's interest in ridding Elsweyr of the Dragons, and that [[Lore:Lorkhaj|Lorkhaj]] returned and fought the darkness back and slayed many Dragons.{{Ref|name=ESOTE|{{Cite Book|Terror from the East}}}}

===Second Era===
====The Hunt Continues====
Although the Dragonguard was officially disbanded at the turn of the Eras,{{ref|name=LOTD|{{Cite Book|Legacy of the Dragonguard}}}} some members continued hunting Dragons until at least {{Year|2E 373}}.{{ref|name=AOD|{{Cite Book|Atlas of Dragons}}}} [[Lore:Grahkrindrog|Grahkrindrog]] was slain by the Dragonguard in {{Year|2E 184}} after perpetrating great slaughter in [[Lore:Winterhold (region)|Winterhold]] and [[Lore:Eastmarch|Eastmarch]] in northeast Skyrim. The Dragon's name was supposedly confirmed to the Dragonguard with assistance from mages of the [[Lore:College of Winterhold|College of Winterhold]]. His death was later documented by the Dragonguard in 2E 373.{{ref|name=AOD|{{Cite Book|Atlas of Dragons}}}} A splinter group known as the [[Lore:Dragonknights|Dragonknights]] would also continue the Akaviri traditions, with the abilities of this group allegedly coming from the Dragons themselves.{{ref|name=LOTD}}{{ref|{{Cite Book|To Smite with Dragon Claws}}}}{{Ref|name=AFDOE|{{Cite Book|Ardent Flame: Draconic or Endemic?}}}} 

The Dragonguard's ''[[Lore:Atlas of Dragons|Atlas of Dragons]]'', compiled in 2E 373, listed [[Lore:Ahbiilok|Ahbiilok]], [[Lore:Mirmulnir|Mirmulnir]], Nahfahlaar, and [[Lore:Paarthurnax|Paarthurnax]] among the Dragons still believed to be alive.{{Ref|name=AOD}}

====A Rage of Dragons ====
[[File:ON-quest-The Halls of Colossus 04.jpg|thumb|left|The Dragons unleashed from the Halls of Colossus]]
After the retrieval of the Wrathstone around {{Year|2E 582}}, the Imperial battlemage [[Lore:Abnur Tharn|Abnur Tharn]] inadvertently unleashed the Dragons from their containment from the Halls of Colossus, thus causing them to continue in their quest for the Moon's Lunar Power. The Dragons allied themselves with the forces of the Queen of [[Lore:Rimmen|Rimmen]], [[Lore:Euraxia Tharn|Euraxia Tharn]], who were led to believe that the Dragons were willing to be used as tools. The Dragons were quick to cross Euraxia when she was no longer of use to them, and they were left without their aid against the forces of Elsweyr Defense Force as well as the [[Lore:The Vestige|Vestige]]. The turn of events resulted in her death and the return of Rimmen to Khajiit control. Euraxia's leaderless forces allied with the Dragons to avoid their wrath. The Vestige subsequently entered the [[Lore:Plane of Jode|Plane of Jode]], slew Mulaamnir, and prevented Kaalgrontiid from absorbing all the energy stored in Jode's Core.{{ref|name=ESOElsweyr|Events of [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Elsweyr|Elsweyr]]''}}{{ref|name=ESOJC|[[ON:Jode's Core (quest)|Jode's Core]] quest in [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Elsweyr|Elsweyr]]''}}

Dragon sightings became more frequent, with many coming out of hiding after learning of Kaalgrontiid's return.{{ref|name=ESODragonRise|Dragon Sightings during the [[Online:Dragon Rise|ESO: Dragon Rise Event]]}} The Dragons [[Lore:Nahviintaas|Nahviintaas]], [[Lore:Yolnahkriin|Yolnahkriin]], and [[Lore:Lokkestiiz |Lokkestiiz]] took residence within [[Lore:Sunspire|Sunspire]], claiming to be the gods [[Lore:Alkosh|Alkosh]], [[Lore:Jone|Jone]], and [[Lore:Jode|Jode]], and converted the majority of the shrine's devotees to their cause. In an attempt to correct the mortal mistake, Nahviintaas plotted to rip the [[Lore:Time Wound|time wound]] left behind from the battle between Alkosh and [[Lore:Pelinal|Pelinal]] long ago. The [[Lore:Undaunted|Undaunted]] learned of this, and fought through the hordes of cultists, and killed each of the false gods.{{Ref|name=ESOElsweyr}} The Dragon Maarselok nested near the mountains within the Tenmar Valley, near the [[Lore:Valenwood|Valenwood]] and Elsweyr border. There, he emitted the dreadful Azureblight from his body, a dangerous blight intended to create an "azure world", where all life would be twisted to his will. Without the aid of the Undaunted and the [[Lore:Changeling|changeling]] [[Lore:Selene|Selene]], the corruption would have spread to the [[Lore:Elden Root|Elden Tree]].{{ref|name=ESOScalebreaker|Events of [[Online:Online|ESO]]: ''[[Online:Scalebreaker|Scalebreaker]]''}}
[[File:ON-quest-New Moon Rising 04.jpg|thumb|right|Nahfahlaar, Abnur Tharn, and the Dragonguard putting an end to the Rage of Dragons]]
The Dragon Laatvulon sought to continue his master Kaalgrontiid's plan of bringing on the Dark Aeon, intending for Kaalgrontiid to become Akatosh's equal.  To this end, Laatvulon created a Khajiiti Dragon cult, the [[Lore:New Moon Cult|New Moon Cult]], whose ranks were bolstered by many of the vulnerable Khajiit from the region, such as those who were affected by the [[Lore:Knahaten Flu|Knahaten Flu]]. 

The red Dragon [[Lore:Nahfahlaar|Nahfahlaar]], who was Laatvulon's ancient rival, dueled Laatvulon near the city of [[Lore:Senchal|Senchal]] in southern Elsweyr, but was overpowered due to Laatvulon's newfound power. Afterwards, taking refuge in [[Lore:Tideholm|Tideholm]], Nahfahlaar was encountered by the Dragonguard, which had been recently reformed by the Redguard Blademaster [[Lore:Sai Sahan|Sai Sahan]]. Seeing that Nahfahlaar meant no harm, an alliance was forged once again with former foes/allies, one in which Nahfahlaar served as a valuable asset. This alliance led to victory, and Laatvulon, the Demon from the East, was slain.{{ref|name=Nahfahlaar}}

Nahfahlaar subsequently aided the Dragonguard in defeating Kaalgrontiid and the remnants of the New Moon Cult. Kaalgrontiid returned and planned to siphon the energy he collected from Jode's Core and become empowered by a giant cluster of [[Lore:Aeonstone|Aeonstone]]. To prevent anyone from disrupting his plans, he utilized the energy of the Aeonstone to create an aegis, a massive and impenetrable ward, around the ancient fortress of [[Lore:Dragonhold|Dragonhold]]. Through the combined efforts of Nahfahlaar, the Dragonguard, and Queen [[Lore:Khamira|Khamira]]'s forces, Kaalgrontiid was slain. However, the Dark Aeon became unstable as a result of Kaalgrontiid's machinations and threatened to destroy the entire continent of Tamriel. Abnur Tharn and Nahfahlaar contained the explosion, limiting its destruction to Dragonhold. Nahfahlaar escaped, but could not confirm whether Tharn survived. After putting a stop to the rage of Dragons, Nahfahlaar sought out another place to wait until his intervention became necessary once again.{{ref|name=Nahfahlaar}}

===The "Disappearance" of the Dragons===
[[File:RG-scene-Introduction_25.jpg|Nafaalilargus, a loyal servant of Tiber Septim|thumb|left]]
{{Quote Box|style=float:none; max-width:none|''There is some confusion over when the last dragon was killed. It seems the last few vanished all at once. Some tales speak of a dragon king who devoured all of them rather than let mankind kill them. One of the more far-fetched stories has Tiber Septim absorbing their essences when he ascended to godhood. Although the exact cause is unknown, they are all gone. No dragon has been seen for centuries. There are a few known examples of dragon bones fused with the stone and rocks of cliffs and caves. Just enough proof to make the stories undeniable.''|''[[Lore:There Be Dragons|There Be Dragons]]''}}
Dragons had continually become more elusive after losing their grip on power back in the Merethic Era.{{ref|name=TBD}}{{ref|name=AOTD|{{Cite Book|Annals of the Dragonguard}}}} Their numbers dwindled over the years due to many causes: much of their population in Akavir was wiped out or enslaved by the Tsaesci, who also brought their onslaught to Tamriel.{{ref|name=MA}} The Akaviri Dragonguard and its successor, the [[Lore:Blades|Blades]], along with other heroes, hunted Dragons and their followers throughout the eras until Dragons were nearly extinct.{{ref|name=OATD}}{{ref|name=AOD|{{Cite Book|Atlas of Dragons}}}}{{ref|name=TBOTD}}

After his rule was established in {{Year|2E 854}},{{ref|name=BHotE1|{{Cite Book|Brief History of the Empire v 1}}}} Emperor [[Lore:Tiber Septim|Tiber Septim]] offered many of the remaining surviving Dragons protection and spoils in return for their fealty.{{ref|name=TBD}}{{ref|name=GN|group=UOL|[[General: Gary Noonan's Posts|Gary Noonan's Posts]]}} Among the Dragons who served him was Nahfahlaar, now known as Nafaalilargus.{{ref|name=RGNafaalilargus|[[RG:Nafaalilargus|Nafaalilargus]]' [[Redguard:Recover the Soul Gem#Conversation with Nafaalilargus|dialogue]] in ''[[RG:Redguard|Redguard]]''}} Circa {{Year|2E 864}}, during the reign of Tiber's [[Lore:Third Empire|Empire]], Dragons were said to circle overhead of the [[Lore:Imperial City|Imperial City]], with River Dragons rusting their hides in its surrounding waters.{{ref|name=PGE1Cyro|{{Cite Book|Pocket Guide to the Empire, 1st Edition/Cyrodiil}}}} 

Despite Tiber's pledge, almost all known wild Dragons were killed or driven off, disappearing from sight in Tamriel.{{ref|name=AOD}}{{ref|name=TBD}} Some sources claim that all Dragons disappeared at once, being devoured by a Dragon king or absorbed by Tiber Septim when he ascended to godhood.{{ref|name=TBD}} Many held the belief that the Dragons were extinct.{{ref|name=Loading}} This was not true, as many live Dragons continued to exist in obscurity throughout the [[Lore:Third Era|Third]] and early [[Lore:Fourth Era|Fourth Era]].
{{NewLine}}

===Third Era===
[[File:DF-npc-Skakmat (face).png|Skakmat, a Dragon known to live in the Third Era|thumb|right]]
====There Be Dragons====
It is believed that, at some time between {{Year|3E 111}} and {{Year|3E 153}}, Sir [[Lore:Amiel Lannus|Amiel Lannus]] and the [[Lore:Knights of the Nine|Knights of the Nine]] traveled to the ruins of  [[Lore:Elenglynn| Elenglynn]] and smote a Wyrm to recover the [[Lore:Cuirass of the Crusader|Cuirass of the Crusader]].{{Ref|name=TKotN|{{Cite Book|The Knights of the Nine}}}}
Nomads in the [[Lore:Alik'r|Alik'r Desert]] frequently spoke of "Great Wyrms" inhabiting its vast wastes, although these claims were never independently confirmed.{{ref|name=DUG|{{Cite Book|Daggerfall User's Guide|ns_base=BK}}}} Various "Dragon's Dens" were known to exist throughout the [[Lore:Iliac Bay|Iliac Bay]] region in the Third Era, littered with charred remains, though it is unclear if Dragons actually took residence within these.{{ref|[[Daggerfall:Dungeons#Dragon's_Den|Dragon's Den]] [[Daggerfall:Dungeons|dungeons]] in ''[[DF:Daggerfall|Daggerfall]]''}}

Many tales tell of Dragons making deals with other races to protect themselves.{{ref|name=AOD}}{{ref|name=TS|{{Cite Book|Twin Secrets}}}} Among known examples, some remained in service to the Empire, possibly residing in the protective custody of secret Imperial strongholds.{{ref|name=GN|group=UOL}} [[Lore:Dragonne Papré|Dragonne Papré]] served as the companion of an [[Lore:Imperial Battlemages|Imperial Battlemage]],{{ref|name=SL|{{Cite Book|Starlover's Log}}}} and it is said the [[Lore:Imperial Guard|Imperial Guard]] as a whole had an elite unit of Dragon riders.{{ref|name=GN|group=UOL|[[General: Gary Noonan's Posts|Gary Noonan's Posts]]}} The [[Lore:Akatosh Chantry|Akatosh Chantry]] held Dragons as their holy symbol, and kept live Dragons (or possibly just [[Lore:Dragonling|Dragonlings]]) in their "sacred groves".{{Ref|name=DFAtD|[[Daggerfall:Avenge the Dragons|Avenge the Dragons]] quest in ''[[Daggerfall:Daggerfall|Daggerfall]]''}}{{ref|name=TAAD}} [[Lore:Skakmat|Skakmat]] is a Dragon who served as the [[Lore:Familiar|familiar]] of the Dowager Queen [[Lore:Nulfaga|Nulfaga]].{{ref|name=DC|[[Books:The Daggerfall Chronicles/Narrative|Daggerfall's Glory Road - The Complete Walkthrough, '''The Daggerfall Chronicles''']] — ''Ronald Wartow''}} Quite notably, Paarthurnax was able to remain in seclusion atop the [[Lore:Throat of the World|Throat of the World]], guarded by the [[Lore:Greybeards|Greybeards]] of [[Lore:High Hrothgar|High Hrothgar]].{{ref|name=Paarthurnax}}{{ref|name=Arngeir}}

===Fourth Era===
====Prelude to Return====
Accounts of surviving Dragons continue into the early Fourth Era. Several Dragons were encountered by [[Lore:The Warrior (person)|the Warrior]] circa {{Year|4E 180}}.{{ref|[[BL:Dragons|Dragons]] in ''[[Blades:Blades|Blades]]''}} Among these was one named [[Lore:Shulkunaak|Shulkunaak]] who assisted the Warrior with information that was essential to defeating the [[Lore:Ayleid|Ayleid]] [[Lore:Lich|lich]] [[Lore:Celemaril|Celemaril the Undying]].{{ref|[[Blades:Facing the Dragon|Facing the Dragon]] quest in ''[[Blades:Blades|Blades]]''}}

====The World-Eater Wakes====
[[File:SR-quest-Dragonslayer.jpg|thumb|right|Alduin  raining down fury during his final stand in Sovngarde]]
In {{Year|4E 201}}, Alduin reappeared at the [[Lore:Throat of the World|Throat of the World]], cast forth from the Time-Wound created when he had been banished thousands of years before. Perched here was his brother, Paarthurnax, who awaited his return at the summit of the Throat of the World ever since his banishment.{{ref|name=Paarthurnax|}} However, Paarthurnax was unable to contain Alduin, and the World-Eater descended upon the region. In the midst of all of this, several members of the [[Lore:Stormcloaks|Stormcloak rebellion]] were being executed by [[Lore:Imperial Legion|Imperial Legionnaires]] in [[Lore:Helgen|Helgen]], including the rebel leader [[Lore:Ulfric Stormcloak|Ulfric Stormcloak]].{{ref|name=SRUnbound|[[SR:Unbound|Unbound]] quest in ''[[SR:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Accidentally caught up in this string of executions was a prisoner who was detained for crossing the border.{{ref|name=SRUnbound|}}

Before this prisoner or Ulfric Stormcloak could be executed, Alduin descended upon Helgen and quickly reduced the settlement to rubble. Several parties were able to narrowly escape the destruction including Ulfric and the prisoner.{{ref|name=SRUnbound|}} Ulfric Stormcloak's resulting escape prolonged the civil war in Skyrim and secured a steady flow of souls for Alduin to consume in Sovngarde.{{ref|name=SRCivil|[[SR:Civil War|Civil War]] questline in ''[[SR:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Alduin began to resurrect his fallen brethren, using a powerful thu'um (seemingly known only to him) to undo their deaths and restore flesh to their bones.{{ref|name=SRBlade|[[Skyrim:A Blade in the Dark|A Blade in the Dark]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Dragon Priests and [[Lore:Draugr|draugr]] began to stir in ancient ruins as well, setting the revitalization of Alduin's army and the Dragon Cult in motion. The mysterious prisoner was eventually revealed to be the [[Lore:Last Dragonborn|Last Dragonborn]], the hero of an [[Lore:Prophecy of the Dragonborn|ancient prophecy]].{{ref|name=SRDragonRising|[[Skyrim:Dragon Rising|Dragon Rising]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}

The [[Lore:Dragonstone|Dragonstone]], an ancient tablet recovered from [[Lore:Bleak Falls Barrow|Bleak Falls Barrow]] in {{Year|4E 201}}, bore a map of Skyrim's Dragon burial sites. An inscription on its reverse anticipated Alduin restoring the fallen Dragons.{{Ref|name=SRDragonstone|[[Skyrim:Dragonstone|Dragonstone]] inscription in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}{{Ref|name=SRBleak|Events of [[Skyrim:Bleak Falls Barrow (quest)|Bleak Falls Barrow]] in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Using the map and her observations of emptied mounds, the Blade [[Lore:Delphine|Delphine]] traced a pattern of resurrections spreading from southeastern Skyrim. She predicted that the mound near [[Lore:Kynesgrove|Kynesgrove]] would be next. There, she and the Last Dragonborn witnessed Alduin restore [[Lore:Sahloknir|Sahloknir]] to life.{{Ref|name=SRDelphine|[[Skyrim:Delphine|Delphine]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}}{{Ref|name=SRBlade}}

As a Dragonborn, the hero was able to wield the thu'um with preternatural ease, learning several powerful shouts that allowed them to slay Alduin's Dragon allies and absorb their souls, increasing the hero's strength and ensuring Alduin could not revive them again. With the aid of an Elder Scroll, the Last Dragonborn learned the forgotten thu'um known as Dragonrend that served as a great weapon against Dragon kind, forcing understanding of mortality into their immortal minds.{{ref|name=Skyrim1|[[Skyrim:Alduin's Bane|Alduin's Bane]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Using Dragonrend, the hero and Paarthurnax defeated Alduin at the Throat of the World, but he proved to be seemingly impossible to slay on Mundus, and so escaped to Sovngarde.{{ref|name=Skyrim1}} 

Eventually, the Dragonborn gained access to Sovngarde.{{ref|name=Skyrim2|[[Skyrim:Dragonslayer|Dragonslayer]] quest in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} Shor gave counsel for the heroes of Sovngarde to stand down, allowing the Last Dragonborn to face Alduin as foretold with the help of the three ancient Nord heroes who banished Alduin in the first place.{{ref|name=SRYsgramor|[[SR:Ysgramor|Ysgramor]]'s dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} With that, the quartet finally vanquished the World-Eater.{{ref|name=Skyrim2|}} Alduin's soul was not absorbed by the Dragonborn, and he may not have been completely destroyed:{{ref|name=Skyrim2|}} it is possible that he will one day return to fulfill his role as the World-Eater.{{ref|name=Arngeir}}

The surviving Dragons had varying reactions to Alduin's vanquishing. Paarthurnax lamented the death of his brother but intended to teach the surviving Dragons the philosophy he had come to adopt, the [[Lore:Way of the Voice|Way of the Voice]].{{ref|name=Paarthurnax}} Some Dragons viewed this attempt at new leadership by Paarthurnax as tyranny, instead bowing to the superiority of the Last Dragonborn, who, as the slayer of Alduin, they felt was the rightful heir to his lordship.{{Ref|name=Odahviing}} Even realms beyond the Mundus felt ripples from the World-Eater's destruction. A Dragon in the [[Lore:Soul Cairn|Soul Cairn]] by the name of [[Lore:Durnehviir|Durnehviir]] learned of the event and proclaimed the Last Dragonborn a true Dovah for the deed.{{ref|name=DBDurnehviir}}

The remaining Dragons would continue to be found throughout Skyrim and Morrowind,{{ref|[[Skyrim:Guard Dialogue#Dragonborn|Redoran Guard]] dialogue in ''[[Skyrim:Skyrim|Skyrim]]''}} though their ultimate fates are unknown.
